::ENG2_U06_L07_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] During rehearsal, a student notices the speaker note says only, "This is good." Which revision best improves oral explanation? {
=A. This proposal would support students who remain after dismissal by giving them a supervised place to complete academic work. #Correct. This revision explains the proposal, the audience need, and the reason clearly.
~B. This is good and everyone should like it. #Incorrect. This is still vague and too casual for a formal presentation.
~C. The slide is blue and has a title. #Incorrect. This describes appearance instead of explaining the message.
~D. I rehearsed and then stopped. #Incorrect. This does not revise the oral explanation.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Feedback says the chart is hard to understand. Which revision best addresses the feedback? {
~A. Add a brighter background behind the chart. #Incorrect. A brighter background may make the data harder to read.
=B. Add a clear chart title, labels, and readable numbers. #Correct. This directly improves the audience's ability to understand the digital evidence.
~C. Remove the evidence and keep the colors. #Incorrect. Removing evidence weakens the presentation.
~D. Read the chart faster during the presentation. #Incorrect. Speed does not solve unclear visual design.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which action shows publication readiness? {
~A. Submitting before checking feedback #Incorrect. Final work should be reviewed before it is shared.
~B. Changing only the font color #Incorrect. A design change alone may not address content, tone, or conventions.
=C. Revising the slide and speaker note so both support the same evidence-based message #Correct. Publication-ready work is aligned, clear, formal, and complete.
~D. Keeping a vague note because the slide has evidence #Incorrect. Oral explanation must still clarify the evidence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which revision improves formal tone? {
~A. "This idea is awesome" to "This idea is really awesome" #Incorrect. The tone remains casual.
~B. "Students need help" to "Kids need stuff" #Incorrect. This makes the wording less formal and less precise.
~C. "The number is cool" to "The number is kind of important" #Incorrect. This remains vague and casual.
=D. "This idea is awesome" to "This proposal would support students' academic focus" #Correct. This revision uses formal, precise language.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is ready for a final speaker note? {
~A. Students needs a supervised place after school. #Incorrect. The plural subject "students" needs the verb "need."
=B. Students need a supervised place after school because many remain on campus after dismissal. #Correct. This sentence is complete and uses standard English conventions.
~C. Because many students remain on campus after dismissal. #Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~D. Students need, a supervised place, after school because. #Incorrect. The commas and ending create an unclear sentence.
}
